\begin_layout Title
Set 6 - Inference: Bootstrap, Clustering, Multi-Way Clustering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Inference in Panel Data Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Clustering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
General Notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Conditional on unbiasedness, we want the most efficient estimator, i.e., the
 estimator with the smallest standard errors.
 This has to do with choosing a specific estimation approach, such as: POLS;
 IV; FE; Controls vs No Controls.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Conditional on choosing an estimator for the treatment effect, we want standard
 error estimates that are correct (not just small).
 So, we want standard error estimates that have us rejecting tests of inference
 null hypotheses an appropriate amount of time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Bertrand, Duflo, Mullainathan (2004, QJE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This paper was a game changer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors considered CPS data on female wages collapsed to state and year
 levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
They randomly chose a year between 1979 and 1999, then randomly assigned
 a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

placebo law
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to 25 states for this chosen year.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The differences-in-differences estimates a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

significant effect
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 45% of the time (at the 5% level).
 So, the standard errors estimates are clearly too small.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Clustering the standard errors on the state led to the appropriate rejection
 rates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
However, there was still overrejection for a few states (11% with 6 states,
 8% with 10 states)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Issue: Correlated Errors (Across Time)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are two approaches to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

correct
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the problem of correlated errors across time
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Modeling (random effects, AR(1), etc.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Perform OLS, then obtain the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

right
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 standard errors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first approach is more efficient, but it imposes additional assumptions
 regarding the standard errors that may not hold.
 The second approach is far more common, and it is the approach most empiricists
 take.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Estimating Standard Errors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How do we estimate standard errors when we allow the errors to be correlated
 within groups? Suppose we have a balanced panel data with 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 Now, consider the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

demeaned
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 FE model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{y}_{it}=\tilde{x}_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\tilde{u}_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

.
 Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{y}_{it} & = & (y_{it}-\bar{y}_{i})\\
\tilde{x}_{it} & = & (x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})\\
\tilde{u}_{it} & = & (u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Also, the fixed effects parameter has been removed as a result of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

demeaning
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 approach.
 Next, stacking over the 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 equations for each observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{y}_{i}=\tilde{x}_{i}\beta+\tilde{u}_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times K$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, stacking over 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{y}=\tilde{X}\beta+\tilde{u}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{u}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $NT\times1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{X}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $NT\times K$
\end_inset

.
 After performing an OLS regression on this equation, we obtain the FE estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\theta} & = & \left(\tilde{X}^{\prime}\tilde{X}\right)^{-1}\tilde{X}^{\prime}\tilde{y}\\
 & = & \left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{y}_{i}\\
 & = & \left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\tilde{x}_{it}\tilde{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\tilde{x}_{it}\tilde{y}_{it}\\
\hat{\theta} & = & \theta+\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{u}_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, assuming independence over 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, we obtain the variance-covariance matrix of the estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V\left[\hat{\beta}\right]=\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}E\left[\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{u}_{i}\tilde{u}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right]\right)\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

We can write the estimator of the variance-covariance matrix as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\widehat{V[\hat{\beta}]}=\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\tilde{u}}_{i}\hat{\tilde{u}}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right)\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\tilde{u}}_{i}=\tilde{y}_{i}-\tilde{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}
\]

\end_inset

This estimator is consistent if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\tilde{u}}_{i}\hat{\tilde{u}}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}E\left[\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{u}_{i}\tilde{u}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Important to note, the finite sample modifications are necessary if we want
 to avoid standard errors that are too small.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Allowing errors to be correlated within groups is analagous to the cross-section
al problem involving heteroskedasticity, except 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a vector, as opposed to a scalar.
 We are relaxing the assumption that the off-diagonal terms are zero.
 Instead of a diagonal variance-covariance matrix, we now have a block diagonal
 matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Clustering
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\sigma_{11,11} & \sigma_{11,12} & \cdots & \sigma_{11,1T}\\
\sigma_{12,11} & \sigma_{12,12} & \cdots & \sigma_{12,1T}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\sigma_{1T,11} & \sigma_{1T,12} & \cdots & \sigma_{1T,1T}
\end{array}\right] &  &  & 0\\
 & \left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\sigma_{21,21} & \sigma_{21,22} & \cdots & \sigma_{21,2T}\\
\sigma_{22,21} & \sigma_{22,22} & \cdots & \sigma_{22,2T}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\sigma_{2T,21} & \sigma_{2T,22} & \cdots & \sigma_{2T,2T}
\end{array}\right]\\
 &  & \ddots\\
0 &  &  & \left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\sigma_{N1,N1} & \sigma_{N1,N2} & \cdots & \sigma_{N1,NT}\\
\sigma_{N2,N1} & \sigma_{N2,N2} & \cdots & \sigma_{N2,NT}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\sigma_{NT,N1} & \sigma_{NT,N2} & \cdots & \sigma_{NT,NT}
\end{array}\right]
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The usual white-heteroskedastic robust approach imposes the stronger assumption
 that all of the off-diagonal terms are zero
\emph on
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{cccccccc}
\sigma_{11} &  &  &  &  &  &  & 0\\
 & \sigma_{12}\\
 &  & \ddots\\
 &  &  & \sigma_{1T}\\
 &  &  &  & \sigma_{21}\\
 &  &  &  &  & \sigma_{22}\\
 &  &  &  &  &  & \ddots\\
0 &  &  &  &  &  &  & \sigma_{N,T}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Assuming homoskedasticity goes even further because it requires that all
 of the diagonal terms are the same.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\sigma^{2} &  &  & 0\\
 & \sigma^{2}\\
 &  & \ddots\\
0 &  &  & \sigma^{2}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Stata
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When performing analysis in Stata, use 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{c}\hat{\tilde{u}}$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\tilde{u}}$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C=\frac{N}{N-1}\cdot\frac{NT-1}{NT-K}\approx\frac{N}{N-1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Stata gets the standard errors wrong with the least squares dummy variable
 estimator (the standard errors are too big because it uses the wrong 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

).
 Though, it does get the standard errors right if you use the mean difference
 estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Important Notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We are especially concerned about correlated errors within groups in FE
 models using panel data, but it is often relevant in other contexts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If we are concerned about correlated errors and groups that are nested,
 then cluster on the more aggregate group.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Example*
Suppose we have multiple observations per state year 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{ist},x_{ist})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we should cluster on the state.
 By doing this, we are allowing the errors to be correlated from one year
 to the next within a state.
 Do not cluster on state-year.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
What if we are concerned about autocorrelation within groups and correlated
 errors across groups in each period? Then, we should use non-nested clustering.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Ask:
\series default
\emph default
 Do our control variables fully capture the sort of clustering that we are
 worrying about?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Suppose we have a state-year panel
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We ususally think of errors that will be correlated within states over time
 because of state fixed effects and persistent shocks that are not common
 to all years.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Including state fixed effects does not remove the correlation in errors.
 We still need to cluster at the state level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We may think that year specific shocks are correlated across states.
 After including year fixed effects, we can ignore this issue if these shocks
 are the same across all states.
 If the shocks are not the same across all states, then we need to do multiway
 clustering (Cameron, Gelbach, and Miller, 2011).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section*

\emph on
Multiway Clustering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Rewrite the middle portion of the variance-covariance estiamtor as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\tilde{u}}_{i}\hat{\tilde{u}}_{j}^{\prime}x_{j}1(i,j\ \mbox{in same cluster})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With multiway clustering, the 
\begin_inset Formula $1()$
\end_inset

 takes a value of one if 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 share any cluster.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To implement multiway clustering, obtain an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{V(\hat{\beta})}$
\end_inset

, clustering on the first dimension, the second dimension, and their interaction.
 Then, add the first two estimates, and subtract the third to account for
 double counting, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\widehat{V_{2W}(\hat{\beta})}=\widehat{V_{1}(\hat{\beta})}+\widehat{V_{2}(\hat{\beta})}-\widehat{V_{int}(\hat{\beta})}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For example, 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the estimate after clustering on states, 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the estimate after clustering on years, and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{int}$
\end_inset

 is the estimate after clustering on the instersection of state and year
 (i.e., errors can only be correlated within state-years)
\emph on
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Asymptotics are based on the number of clusters of the dimension that is
 the fewest in number.
 Also, note that multiway clustering may be inappropriate for small 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 and/or 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Small Number of Individuals/Groups/Clusters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We need to adjust the standard error estimates because they are too small.
 So, we use modified residuals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The test statistics are not standard normally distributed
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If we perform inference using standard critical values, then we overreject.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Two possible solutions:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Use a t-distribution.
 It is not clear what t-distribution should be used.
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{N-1}$
\end_inset

? Some argue that more adjustment is needed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bootstrapping with asymptotic refinement
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section*
Bootstrapping
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The problem with inference is that we do not know what the distribution
 of sample statistics looks like because we only observe one sample statistic.
 The bootstrap solution is to learn about this distribution by taking samples
 of our sample, and then consider what the distribution of sample statistics
 looks like based on these 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pseudosamples.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 We can use these 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pseudosamples
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to obtain the distribution of the coefficient, t-statistics, standard errors,
 or a combined coefficient.
 Though, this is usually not needed because we have theoretical information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The two main uses of bootstrapping are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Estimating standard errors when analytic formulas are complex, or the formula
 cannot be calculated, e.g., the Hausman test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Asymptotic refinement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Lindo (2009)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The author considered the following model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Birth}_{it}=\alpha_{i}+\alpha_{t}+\theta_{0}1(\mbox{YearHusbandDisplaced)}+\theta_{1}1(\mbox{YearHusbandDisplaced}+1)+\cdots+\theta_{6}(\mbox{YearHusbandDisplaced}+6)+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

He wanted to know the average cumulative effect, but he was not able to
 just sum 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 because the panel was not balanced.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Estimate the model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Obtain predicted probabilities for all post periods (for treated women)
 and sum
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This gives the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

true predicted number of kids in the post periods
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Turn off treatment indicators and repeat the previous procedure
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This gives the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

counterfactual predicted number of kids in the post periods
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The average difference provides ATET of impact on completed fertility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Basic Bootstrapping
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we have a sample size of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, and we are interested in statistic 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Form a pseudosample of size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 by drawing from the original sample with replacement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Produce pseudosample statistic, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Repeat this procedure 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 times, in order to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}^{*},\ldots,\hat{\beta}_{B}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
These pseudosample statistics will be centered around 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

, which was produced by the full sample.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The stardard deviation of this set of pseudosample statistics is our bootstrap
 estimate of the standard error of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For inference, we can construct test statistics using this standard error
 and critical values from the standard normal or 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 distributions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This approach does not improve finite sample performance.
 It relies on asymptotic theory for inference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
There are many different 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

types
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of bootstraps.
 The one covered above is a non-parametric bootstrap, also known as a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

paired bootsrap
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 because it resamples 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,x)$
\end_inset

 pairs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Bootstrapping With Asymptotic Refinement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use the bootstrap approach to estimate the distribution of the t-statistic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For each iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

, calculate the test statistic
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t_{b}=\frac{\hat{\beta}_{b}-\hat{\beta}}{SE_{\hat{\beta}_{b}^{*}}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The empirical distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}^{*},\ldots,\ t_{B}^{*}$
\end_inset

 estimates the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 at level 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $t<t_{\nicefrac{\alpha}{2}}^{*}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $t>t_{1-\nicefrac{\alpha}{2}}^{*}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{q}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the qth quantile of 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}^{*},\ldots,\ t_{B}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This gives us critical values to compare our test statistics too, or we
 can use our t-statistic with this distribution to compute our p-value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This approach provides asyptotic refinement because it has us compare our
 test statistic with the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

right
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 distribution.
 This works with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

asymptotically pivotal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 statistics, those whose limit distribution does not depend on unknown parameter
s.
 This is not the case for most estimators, but will be with asympotically
 normal or 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 test statistics and the confidence intervals they imply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Cluster Bootstrap
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Recall that asymptotics are based on the number of clusters approaching
 infinity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Draw clusters with replacement, with the number of draws equal to the number
 of clusters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Few clusters and regressors that take few values
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
E.g.
 treatment indicator 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Reference: Comeron et al.
 (2008 REStat)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Pairs cluster bootstrap-t overrejects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In contrast, wild cluster bootstrap-t works well.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
Wild Cluster Bootstrap-t
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Short Version
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Instead of drawing observations 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,x)$
\end_inset

, we draw 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and estimated errors, generate predicted 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 using these errors and our estimated 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and then regress these predicted 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

's on the actual 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

's.
 What does this accomplish? It preserves the within-group correlation in
 errors observed in the full sample when generating new estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Recipe
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{g}$
\end_inset

 (imposing 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Then, for each bootstrap iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 (with a total of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 iterations):
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Draw 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{g},\hat{u}_{g})$
\end_inset

 with replacement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Form 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{g}^{*}=\hat{u}_{g}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{g}^{*}=\hat{u}_{g}$
\end_inset

 with probability 0.5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Then, form 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{g}^{*}=x_{g}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}+\hat{u}_{g}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We then have a cluster bootstrap resample: 
\begin_inset Formula $\{(\hat{y}_{1}^{*},x_{1}),\ldots,(\hat{y}_{G}^{*},x_{G})\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use the resample to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{b}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{SE}_{\hat{\beta}_{b}^{*}}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, construct the t-statistic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use this distribution of the test statistic for inference with asymptotic
 refinement
\end_layout

